1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,524
The difference between a useless AI answer

2
00:00:02,584 --> 00:00:05,048
and a brilliant one is usually four small

3
00:00:05,108 --> 00:00:06,430
parts in your message.

4
00:00:06,490 --> 00:00:08,321
Most people only use one of them.

5
00:00:08,376 --> 00:00:11,052
We're going to build a prompt from scratch,

6
00:00:11,114 --> 00:00:12,742
one block at a time,

7
00:00:12,824 --> 00:00:16,036
and watch the answer get better with every

8
00:00:16,113 --> 00:00:17,955
block. And at the end of this,

9
00:00:18,012 --> 00:00:20,614
I will share with you a trick that basically

10
00:00:20,674 --> 00:00:22,625
writes the entire prompt for you.

11
00:00:23,035 --> 00:00:26,572
The task. This is the one block everyone gets

12
00:00:26,640 --> 00:00:29,375
right. Most people just prompt AI with,

13
00:00:29,446 --> 00:00:31,725
"Write a birthday message for my mum," and

14
00:00:31,779 --> 00:00:33,407
would get back something like,

15
00:00:33,461 --> 00:00:35,235
"Thank you for the love,

16
00:00:35,309 --> 00:00:38,084
the patience, and all the times you believed

17
00:00:38,149 --> 00:00:41,069
in me before I believed in myself." It's fine,

18
00:00:41,126 --> 00:00:43,952
but it's also a greeting card from a petrol

19
00:00:44,018 --> 00:00:46,874
station. Write a birthday message is a task.

20
00:00:46,939 --> 00:00:48,761
Everyone gets this block.

21
00:00:48,834 --> 00:00:51,915
It's the other three that makes the difference.

22
00:00:52,330 --> 00:00:54,390
Block two is the context,

23
00:00:54,472 --> 00:00:57,025
the stuff that's in your head that AI can't

24
00:00:57,084 --> 00:01:00,367
see. For example, my mum's turning sixty.

25
00:01:00,445 --> 00:01:03,274
She just retired from thirty years as a nurse.

26
00:01:03,336 --> 00:01:05,992
She's funny, a bit sarcastic,

27
00:01:06,080 --> 00:01:08,964
and she would cringe at anything too soppy.

28
00:01:09,031 --> 00:01:10,798
Now look at the response.

29
00:01:10,869 --> 00:01:13,129
The NHS will have to find someone else to

30
00:01:13,184 --> 00:01:15,113
give that look to patients who say,

31
00:01:15,168 --> 00:01:18,655
"It's probably nothing." Suddenly it's about

32
00:01:18,748 --> 00:01:21,570
her, the nursing, the retirement,

33
00:01:21,653 --> 00:01:23,042
the sense of humor.

34
00:01:23,115 --> 00:01:26,457
It's the same AI. The difference is the context

35
00:01:26,518 --> 00:01:28,844
you provided. Block three,

36
00:01:28,938 --> 00:01:32,582
the rules. This covers things like length,

37
00:01:32,661 --> 00:01:34,836
tone, what to avoid.

38
00:01:34,923 --> 00:01:38,285
For example, keep it under fifty words.

39
00:01:38,365 --> 00:01:39,674
It's going in a card,

40
00:01:39,737 --> 00:01:42,706
so no emojis, and don't use the word queen,

41
00:01:42,770 --> 00:01:45,348
'cause you'd be surprised at how much AI wants

42
00:01:45,404 --> 00:01:46,693
to call your mum queen.

43
00:01:46,749 --> 00:01:49,671
The rules stop the stuff you delete anyways.

44
00:01:49,737 --> 00:01:52,358
Now the AI response is short and card-ready.

45
00:01:52,418 --> 00:01:54,741
Thirty years of bossing doctors around,

46
00:01:54,800 --> 00:01:57,769
and now you're free to boss us full time.

47
00:01:58,191 --> 00:02:00,660
Block four, the examples.

48
00:02:00,761 --> 00:02:03,059
Don't just describe a message you want.

49
00:02:03,118 --> 00:02:04,170
Provide an example.

50
00:02:04,225 --> 00:02:06,328
This is the one most people don't use,

51
00:02:06,383 --> 00:02:08,858
and it's probably the most powerful.

52
00:02:08,927 --> 00:02:11,212
If you have a specific style that you want

53
00:02:11,267 --> 00:02:13,770
the AI response to mirror and draw inspiration

54
00:02:13,824 --> 00:02:16,253
from, provide it. Like,

55
00:02:16,446 --> 00:02:19,393
"Here's the birthday message I wrote for my

56
00:02:19,461 --> 00:02:22,130
dad this year. Use it as a style and length

57
00:02:22,189 --> 00:02:25,288
example." Now look at a portion of the response

58
00:02:25,355 --> 00:02:28,019
that came back. "Thanks for every plaster,

59
00:02:28,080 --> 00:02:31,384
every pep talk, and every I told you so.

60
00:02:31,462 --> 00:02:35,073
Love you. Don't get soppy." Same kind of list,

61
00:02:35,134 --> 00:02:36,713
same kind of sign-off,

62
00:02:36,785 --> 00:02:39,841
same length. You never had to explain your

63
00:02:39,906 --> 00:02:42,776
style. Telling AI be casual is vague.

64
00:02:42,857 --> 00:02:44,681
Showing it an example,

65
00:02:44,764 --> 00:02:46,314
that's a blueprint.

66
00:02:46,746 --> 00:02:48,590
Put them all together and compare.

67
00:02:48,644 --> 00:02:50,968
The first message could be for anyone.

68
00:02:51,029 --> 00:02:54,039
The new one could only be for your mum.

69
00:02:54,116 --> 00:02:56,718
Usually, context. And as someone who works

70
00:02:56,782 --> 00:02:59,386
with AI every day as a software engineer,

71
00:02:59,450 --> 00:03:02,565
I'll be honest, I still catch myself writing

72
00:03:02,630 --> 00:03:04,141
lazy one-liner prompts.

73
00:03:04,207 --> 00:03:06,502
Then I wonder why the answer is lazy.

74
00:03:06,564 --> 00:03:09,084
Garbage in, garbage out.

75
00:03:09,552 --> 00:03:11,869
Okay, the trick I promised.

76
00:03:11,944 --> 00:03:14,354
If you don't know what context to give,

77
00:03:14,416 --> 00:03:17,384
make AI figure it out and request it from

78
00:03:17,456 --> 00:03:20,412
you. Add this one line to any prompt.

79
00:03:20,493 --> 00:03:24,009
Before you answer, ask me questions you need.

80
00:03:24,092 --> 00:03:25,690
Instead of AI guessing,

81
00:03:25,760 --> 00:03:28,316
it responds with clarifying questions like,

82
00:03:28,375 --> 00:03:30,442
What tone? How long?

83
00:03:30,557 --> 00:03:32,214
Is it a milestone birthday?

84
00:03:32,275 --> 00:03:34,711
Card, text, or speech?

85
00:03:34,816 --> 00:03:36,785
How do you usually sign off?

86
00:03:36,855 --> 00:03:38,685
You answer and boom,

87
00:03:38,777 --> 00:03:40,890
all four blocks filled in,

88
00:03:40,971 --> 00:03:43,356
and your final prompt produces a response

89
00:03:43,415 --> 00:03:45,802
you can work with. So that's it.

90
00:03:45,874 --> 00:03:47,541
The task, the context,

91
00:03:47,617 --> 00:03:49,759
the rules, and an example.

92
00:03:49,830 --> 00:03:52,139
You don't need all four all the time.

93
00:03:52,201 --> 00:03:54,700
Asking what's the time in Tokyo doesn't need

94
00:03:54,757 --> 00:03:57,293
your life story. But for anything personal

95
00:03:57,356 --> 00:03:59,618
that doesn't have a binary response,

96
00:03:59,681 --> 00:04:01,374
give it the four blocks.

97
00:04:01,795 --> 00:04:04,113
For a prompt that you use more frequently,

98
00:04:04,168 --> 00:04:06,676
you can create a skill around that task so

99
00:04:06,736 --> 00:04:09,543
that you don't have to type out the four blocks

100
00:04:09,602 --> 00:04:11,775
every single time. In future videos,

101
00:04:11,836 --> 00:04:14,523
I will show you how to turn your best prompt

102
00:04:14,585 --> 00:04:15,989
into a skill. I gotchu.

